ssum\'s Book of Practical Cats."', 'similarity score': 0.645682156085968}, {'frames/frame_0156': 'frames/frame_0156', 'text': 'The text, written in a white handwritten-style font, is superimposed over a dark purple background and reads: "to the poet who first breathed life into these feline creatures. Each songs lyrics are the direct text of the poems by T.S. Eliot in the book titled Old Possum\'s Book of Practical Cats. All this being said, the tale of CATS now becomes two stories to tell, that which Andrew Lloyd Webber put to music and the original intent of T.S. Eliot. To tell our version of this story you, the audience, need to travel back to the time of T.S. Eliot to feel the true intention of the poems themselves. The story of CATS truly begins with the reason T.S. Eliot wrote Old Possum\'s Book of Practical Cats."', 'similarity score': 0.6450886726379395}, {'462': '462', 'text': 'frames/frame_0463 seconds: The image shows a man sitting at a desk on a stage, with a large purple background and white text overlaying it.\n\n*   The man is wearing a suit and tie, and is seated at a small wooden desk.\n    *   He has short dark hair and is looking down at a piece of paper in front of him.\n    *   His hands are resting on the desk, with his right hand holding a pen.\n*   The background is a large purple screen with a faint outline of a cat\'s face and other designs.\n    *   The screen is lit from behind, creating a glow effect around the man.\n    *   The overall atmosphere suggests a theatrical or performance setting.\n*   The white text overlays the image, providing context or information about the scene.\n    *   The text is written in a cursive font and appears to be discussing the meaning of the word "Jellicle".\n    *   It mentions that Jellicle can be used as a descriptive word placed in front of any other word, and that each cat tells its own story in song and dance, hoping to be chosen as the special cat to come back to a different Jellicle life.\n\nOvera